Ladybird prefers artwork entries closest to 512x512, square ones
first on a tie, and tries the next entry when one fails to load or
decode. WebKit’s MediaMetadata::imageDimensionsScore() also favors
square artwork near 512px, and Gecko’s MediaMetadata::FetchArtwork()
also moves on to the next entry after a failure, though Gecko ranks
entries by declared area (GetMediaArtworkFetchOrder()). Chromium’s
MediaImageManager::SelectImage() aims for 150px, which is the size of
its notification thumbnail.

All three engines fetch artwork in the content process, in no-cors mode
with credentials, as Ladybird does, and hand the platform a decoded
image. Ladybird scales it down to at most 512px. Chromium resizes it to
150px (FilterAndResizeImagesForMaximalSize()), while Gecko and WebKit
pass it on at its decoded size.
